                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This one is an acquired taste and I cannot really imagine who wants to acquire said taste. I'll give it this much: this is not a game meant to appeal to the masses. It is very much on the flipside, clearly having being built to appeal to its developers and likely no one else. The gameplay is beyond dated for its release time, and goes beyond simple inexperience, this is a conscious choice. It demands willing engagement with NPCs that are as incoherent as they are inconsequential, and the game very much wants to try and get you to be in their state as well. Any gameplay path you decide to take, be it interest in discovering its mysteries, fighting your way to communal dominance, or trying to immerse yourself in the role of a doctor trying to prevent a plague, the game will try to dissuade you from doing. Everything you may want to do here is as deliberately obtuse, janky and unfriendly to you, the person who actually spent your money on this, as it can get. I admire the \"no handholding\" approach, but I have yet to see another game that tries to beat me over the head with a metal chair for daring to have hands in the first place. I hated almost everything about it, I will never start it up again, I feel like my time was absolutely wasted, but I do admire that the developers had its vision for something I hate that much. This is literal \"if it causes you to have that much emotion, it must be some sort of art\" and I give it that much credit. I would still very much recommend this game *only* to people who are very, very specific. \n\nThis is Russian Literature: The Game, and I would only recommend it to people that have a pathologic enfatuation with this very specific genre.",
